          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How often does guidance describe checking thyroid function once treatment is stable?",
          "answer": "Guidance in this corpus describes checking the thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) level about every 6 to 12 months once treatment is stable. This comes from the Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance, \"Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change.\" It applies to people on levothyroxine for an underactive thyroid, and the passages say the level must be stable on an unchanged dose.\n\nThe same guidance says the test is first repeated 6 to 8 weeks after starting levothyroxine or after any dose change. It explains that an earlier test still reflects the old dose and can lead to unnecessary further changes.\n\nThe retrieved material shows no disagreement between authorities about this monitoring interval. The disagreement the tools found concerns whether to treat mildly raised TSH (subclinical hypothyroidism), not how often to test. Some guidance favours a trial of treatment when symptoms, antibodies or cardiovascular risk are present, while other guidance favours monitoring alone for most people.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not cover antithyroid medicines, so I cannot say what guidance recommends for monitoring those. Your own testing schedule depends on your situation, so it is worth asking your clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
